Now write src/index.js. I need a selector parser that handles:
- ids (#a)
- classes (.b), attribute selectors ([href]), pseudo-classes (:hover, :not(...), :is(...), :where(...))
- elements (ul, li) and pseudo-elements (::before, ::marker, also legacy single-colon like :before, :after, :first-line, :first-letter)

Handling :not and :is by taking max specificity of arguments; :where contributes zero.

I'll write a tokenizer/parser that splits on combinators at top level and processes compound selectors.

Simple approach: tokenize the selector into compound sequences separated by combinators (whitespace, >, +, ~). For each compound, parse simple selectors. For functional pseudo-classes with arguments (:not, :is, :where, :has?), parse inner selectors — need balanced parenthesis splitting. Per spec, :not() takes a selector list; specificity is the most specific selector in its argument list. Same for :is(). :where = 0. :has — modern spec: specificity of most specific argument too, but not required; I can include it for robustness.

Parsing inner lists: split on commas at paren-depth 0, then recurse on each.

Pseudo-elements: ::name → c. Legacy single-colon names (before, after, first-line, first-letter) → treat as pseudo-elements per spec. Also note pseudo-elements can't have args typically.

Element parsing within compound: an identifier at start of compound that isn't prefixed → element. Universal * contributes nothing.

Let me write it carefully.

Tokenizing compounds: scan characters, track bracket depth for [ ] and parens for ( ). Split points: combinator chars > + ~ outside brackets/parens; whitespace outside also acts as descendant combinator unless adjacent to >+~.

